From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 17:49:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 276/277] virtual: a protection change on a page of ntdll's
 data copies only what was written while it was open, into the caller's copy

NtProtectVirtualMemory on a page of an image that runs from a JIT-pool
copy copied the image's page over the copy's, on every call, and into
the first copy of the image it found. For the loader's own pattern
(open a page PAGE_READWRITE, write an import table or ARM64EC metadata
slots at the image's address, restore it) that brings the writes in.
But ntdll's code keeps its globals in its copy, where the image's page
is stale, and the loader never patches ntdll's image: a guest's
VirtualProtect of a page of ntdll's .data (World of Warcraft's x64
client hooks Wow64PrepareForException there; the page also holds the
loader's hash_table, node_ntdll and tls_dirs) put the stale bytes over
the live ones, twice (open and restore), and into the session's copy of
ntdll rather than the calling child's: the exchook probe's sibling
process then crashed in find_basename_module (runs 20261101T160121,
20261101T161254).

For a page of ntdll's writable sections, opening it (PAGE_READWRITE,
PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE) now keeps the image's bytes and copies nothing;
restoring it copies only the 8-byte words written in between, and the
relocation and pointer passes touch only those words (pp_image_sync.h).
Every other module's page is copied whole as before (applying the merge
to them hung the session's start: the loader's metadata writes need
the whole page). The copy synced is the calling process's own (a
child's ntdll), else the shared one, as address translation resolves
it. The sync's lines print with the other protection-change lines (the
first 64, then every 65536th).

Linear: PLA-516, PLA-514
Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-11-01-wow-x64-fex.md
Offered-upstream: no; the pool copy is Madeira's iOS JIT design
---
